# ECG Basics (Qazi) — Version 1, 15 items (6 with an ECG)
Q = [
dict(img=None,
stem="A 64-year-old woman has a routine ECG before cataract surgery. She feels well and takes no medications. The ECG is recorded at the standard paper speed of 25 mm/s. The rhythm is regular at 70/min with an upright P wave in lead II before every QRS complex. The interval from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex measures 6 small boxes on every beat, and no beats are dropped.{FIG}Which of the following is this patient's PR interval?",
choices=["60 ms","120 ms","240 ms","300 ms","1200 ms"], key=2,
exp="At 25 mm/s: <b>1 small box = 1 mm = 0.04 s (40 ms)</b>, 1 big box = 5 small = 0.20 s (200 ms), and 5 big boxes = 1 second (a standard 12-lead covers 10 s).<br><br>6 small boxes × 40 ms = <b>240 ms</b>. The normal PR is <b>200 ms or less (one big box)</b>, so a constant PR of 240 ms with every P conducted = <b>first-degree AV block</b>. It is slowed conduction through the AV node, and every P still gets through.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> At 25 mm/s one small box is 40 ms and one large box is 200 ms. A constant PR over 200 ms with every P conducted is first-degree AV block.",
wrong={0:"60 ms uses 10 ms per small box. At standard speed each small box is 40 ms.",
1:"120 ms uses 20 ms per box, half the true value. 120 ms is the lower limit of a normal PR, not this measurement.",
3:"300 ms uses 50 ms per box. The correct value is 40 ms (0.04 s) per small box.",
4:"1200 ms treats each small box as a big box (200 ms). That would mean a PR longer than a second."},
eli5="On ECG paper each tiny square is 0.04 seconds. Six tiny squares is 0.24 seconds, which is 240 milliseconds. That's a bit longer than the normal limit of 200, so the signal is slow getting through the AV node."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 72-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of palpitations for 1 day. A standard 12-lead ECG is recorded. The rhythm is irregularly irregular with no discernible P waves. You count 17 QRS complexes on the lead II rhythm strip at the bottom of the tracing.{FIG}Which of the following is the best estimate of this patient's ventricular rate?",
choices=["51/min","85/min","102/min","170/min","204/min"], key=2,
exp="Dr. Qazi's method works for <b>any</b> rhythm, regular or not: a standard 12-lead's bottom rhythm strip is <b>10 seconds</b>, so <b>count every QRS × 6</b>. 17 × 6 = <b>102/min</b>. That is atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response (tachycardia is 100 or more).<br><br>The \"300–150–100–75–60–50\" big-box method only works for <b>regular</b> rhythms, so it fails in atrial fibrillation. A 6-second monitor strip would be × 10. The machine's rate is right about 90% of the time, but artifact fools it.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Ventricular rate on a 12-lead = QRS complexes on the 10-second rhythm strip × 6. This works even for irregular rhythms such as atrial fibrillation.",
wrong={0:"51/min multiplies by 3, as if the strip were 20 seconds long. A standard 12-lead is 10 seconds.",
1:"85/min multiplies by 5, which corresponds to a 12-second strip.",
3:"170/min multiplies by 10, which is the rule for a <b>6-second</b> monitor strip, not a 10-second 12-lead.",
4:"204/min multiplies by 12, as if the strip were 5 seconds long."},
eli5="The long strip at the bottom of an ECG covers 10 seconds. Count the heartbeats and multiply by 6 to get beats per minute. 17 times 6 is 102."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 30-year-old man has an ECG during a pre-employment physical examination. He feels well. His pulse is 64/min and regular. The ECG shows a P wave before every QRS complex with a constant PR interval of 130 ms and regular P–P and R–R intervals. The QRS duration is 90 ms.{FIG}Which of the following additional findings would indicate that this rhythm is NOT originating from the sinoatrial node?",
choices=["Biphasic P wave in lead V1","Heart rate increasing to 104/min with exercise","Inverted P wave in lead aVR","Inverted P waves in lead II","PR interval that remains constant at 130 ms"], key=3,
exp="Dr. Qazi's <b>five criteria for sinus rhythm</b>, all of which must be true: regular R–R, regular P–P, constant PR, a P for every QRS, and a <b>P upright in lead II</b>.<br><br>The SA node sits high in the right atrium, so atrial depolarization travels <b>down and to the left</b>, toward lead II → an upright P in II. An <b>inverted P in II</b> means the atria are activated from below (a low atrial or junctional focus), so the rhythm is <b>not sinus</b>, even if everything else is regular.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Sinus rhythm requires an upright P wave in lead II (plus regular P–P and R–R, a constant PR, and a P for every QRS). An inverted P in II means an ectopic atrial or junctional origin.",
wrong={0:"A biphasic P in V1 is <b>normal</b>: the right atrium depolarizes first (positive), then the left atrium (negative).",
1:"A sinus node that speeds up to 104 with exercise is still sinus. It is simply <b>sinus tachycardia</b> and must be called that.",
2:"The P wave is normally <b>inverted in aVR</b>, because that lead looks at the heart from the right shoulder, opposite to atrial depolarization.",
4:"A constant PR is one of the five sinus criteria. It supports sinus rhythm."},
eli5="The heart's natural pacemaker sits at the top right, so its signal travels down toward lead II and makes an upright P wave. If the P wave in lead II points down, the signal must be starting somewhere lower. So it isn't a normal sinus rhythm."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 78-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is seen for a routine visit. She feels at her baseline. Her pulse is 82/min and slightly irregular. A 12-lead ECG shows a narrow-complex irregular rhythm with a distinct P wave before every QRS complex. At least three different P-wave shapes are present, and the PR interval varies with the P-wave shape.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely rhythm?",
choices=["Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Second-degree AV block, Mobitz I","Sinus arrhythmia","Wandering atrial pacemaker"], key=4,
exp="Three or more P-wave morphologies = impulses from <b>several atrial foci</b>. The name depends only on the <b>rate</b>:<br>• Under 100 → <b>wandering atrial pacemaker</b>.<br>• 100 or more → <b>multifocal atrial tachycardia</b> (classic in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease).<br>Her rate is 82, so this is a wandering atrial pacemaker. These are two of Dr. Qazi's five irregular rhythms, with atrial fibrillation, sinus arrhythmia and second-degree AV block.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> At least three P-wave shapes with a rate under 100 is a wandering atrial pacemaker. At 100 or more it is multifocal atrial tachycardia.",
wrong={0:"Atrial fibrillation has <b>no discrete P waves</b>, just an undulating baseline. She has a distinct P before every QRS.",
1:"Multifocal atrial tachycardia has the same multiple P shapes, but the rate must be <b>100 or more</b>. Hers is 82.",
2:"Mobitz I has one P shape, a progressively lengthening PR, and a dropped QRS. Here the PR varies because the P waves come from different foci.",
3:"Sinus arrhythmia has <b>one</b> P shape (all sinus), with the rate varying with breathing."},
eli5="Several different spots in her upper heart chambers take turns starting the beat, so the P waves come in at least three shapes. Because her heart rate is under 100, it's called a wandering atrial pacemaker. At 100 or more it would be multifocal atrial tachycardia."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 66-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of palpitations. An ECG shows a regular narrow-complex rhythm with a ventricular rate of 75/min and a continuous sawtooth pattern of atrial activity at 300/min, best seen in leads II, III, and aVF. He is referred for catheter ablation of the circuit responsible for this rhythm.{FIG}The ablation will most likely target which of the following locations?",
choices=["Accessory pathway between atrium and ventricle","Atrioventricular node","Cavotricuspid isthmus of the right atrium","Pulmonary vein ostia of the left atrium","Sinoatrial node"], key=2,
exp="Sawtooth atrial waves at ~300/min in II, III and aVF = <b>atrial flutter</b>, here with <b>4:1 conduction</b> (300 ÷ 4 = 75). Dr. Qazi calls flutter \"highly tested.\"<br><br>Typical flutter is a single <b>macro-re-entry circuit in the right atrium</b> that runs around the tricuspid annulus through the <b>cavotricuspid isthmus</b>, the strip of tissue between the inferior vena cava and the tricuspid valve. Ablating a line across the isthmus cuts the circuit and cures typical flutter in most patients. <b>Atrial fibrillation</b>, in contrast, starts around the <b>pulmonary veins in the left atrium</b>.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Typical atrial flutter is a right atrial re-entry circuit through the cavotricuspid isthmus, where ablation targets it. Atrial fibrillation originates around the pulmonary veins.",
wrong={0:"An accessory pathway is the substrate of <b>Wolff–Parkinson–White</b> (short PR, delta wave), not a sawtooth atrial rhythm.",
1:"The AV node is the circuit in <b>AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia</b>. Ablating the node itself would cause complete heart block.",
3:"Pulmonary vein isolation treats <b>atrial fibrillation</b>, which has no organized sawtooth waves.",
4:"The sinoatrial node generates sinus rhythm. Flutter is a re-entry loop that overrides it, not a sinus node problem."},
eli5="In atrial flutter an electrical signal runs round and round a loop in the right upper chamber. The loop always passes through a narrow bridge of tissue near the tricuspid valve. Burning a line across that bridge breaks the loop."),

dict(img='klad', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 62-year-old woman has an ECG before a colonoscopy. She has no symptoms, no history of heart disease, and has never had a myocardial infarction. Blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Echocardiography last year showed normal wall thickness and function. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely explanation for the axis on this ECG?",
choices=["Left anterior fascicular block","Left bundle branch block","Left posterior fascicular block","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Old inferior myocardial infarction"], key=0,
exp="Axis in two seconds (Dr. Qazi): look at <b>lead I and aVF</b>. I up + aVF down → the peaks point away from each other → <b>left axis deviation</b> (confirmed by a negative lead II). Then ask \"why?\"<br><br>Her four causes of left axis:<br>• <b>Left ventricular hypertrophy</b>: excluded (normal wall thickness, normal voltage).<br>• <b>Left bundle branch block</b>: excluded (the QRS is narrow, under 120 ms).<br>• <b>Old inferior infarction</b>: excluded (no pathologic Q waves in II, III and aVF, and no history of infarction).<br>• <b>Left anterior fascicular block</b>: a left axis with <b>no other explanation</b> is LAFB, which is common on its own.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Left axis deviation (I positive, aVF negative) with a narrow QRS and no LVH, left bundle branch block or inferior Q waves is left anterior fascicular block.",
wrong={1:"Left bundle branch block gives a left axis, but the QRS must be 120 ms or more. Hers is narrow.",
2:"Left posterior fascicular block shifts the axis to the <b>right</b> (I negative, aVF positive), and it is rare alone.",
3:"Left ventricular hypertrophy needs high voltages and usually a thick wall. Her echocardiogram was normal.",
4:"An old inferior infarct makes aVF negative through <b>Q waves</b>. She has no inferior Q waves and has never had an infarction."},
eli5="The QRS points up in lead I and down in aVF, so the heart's electrical direction has swung left. There's no thick muscle, no wide bundle block and no old heart attack to explain it. The leftover cause is a block in one small branch of the left wiring, the anterior fascicle."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 71-year-old man with a history of hypertension has an ECG before knee replacement surgery. He has no cardiac symptoms. The ECG shows sinus rhythm at 68/min. The QRS duration is 140 ms with an rSR′ pattern in V1 and V2 and slurred S waves in I, aVL, and V6. The QRS is negative in lead I and positive in aVF. Echocardiography shows normal right ventricular size and wall thickness, and there are no Q waves in I or aVL.{FIG}Which of the following best describes this patient's conduction abnormality?",
choices=["Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block alone","Right bundle branch block with left anterior fascicular block","Right bundle branch block with left posterior fascicular block","Right ventricular hypertrophy"], key=3,
exp="Two separate findings:<br>• <b>Right bundle branch block</b>: QRS 120 ms or more, rSR′ (\"rabbit ears\") in V1–V2, slurred S in I, aVL and V6 (\"MaRRoW\": M in V1, W in V6).<br>• <b>Right axis deviation</b>: I negative, aVF positive (the peaks point toward each other).<br><br>Dr. Qazi's \"classic wrong answer\": <b>right bundle branch block has a normal axis</b>. So a right axis needs its own cause. Her three causes of right axis are right ventricular hypertrophy, left posterior fascicular block and old lateral infarction. Here there is no RVH (normal echocardiogram) and no lateral Q waves, which leaves <b>left posterior fascicular block</b>. RBBB + right axis = <b>RBBB + LPFB</b>.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right bundle branch block does not shift the axis. RBBB with right axis deviation and no RVH or lateral infarct means an added left posterior fascicular block.",
wrong={0:"Left bundle branch block makes V1 mostly <b>negative</b> with broad notched R waves laterally and a left axis. He has an rSR′ in V1.",
1:"Right bundle branch block alone has a <b>normal</b> axis. His axis is rightward, so a second abnormality is present.",
2:"Left anterior fascicular block would add a <b>left</b> axis (I positive, aVF negative), which is the opposite of his.",
4:"Right ventricular hypertrophy causes a right axis with tall R waves in V1, but his echocardiogram shows a normal right ventricle."},
eli5="He has a block in the right branch of the heart's wiring, which makes the rabbit-ear pattern. That alone shouldn't swing the axis, but his axis points right. So a second block must be present, in the back branch of the left wiring."),

dict(img='kmob2', cap='Lead II rhythm strip, 25 mm/s',
stem="An 83-year-old man comes to the office because of two episodes of lightheadedness and near-fainting in the past week. He takes no AV-nodal–blocking drugs. His pulse is 46/min. A rhythm strip is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate management?",
choices=["Adenosine injection","Oral metoprolol","Permanent pacemaker placement","Reassurance and observation","Stopping caffeine intake"], key=2,
exp="Regular P–P, an irregular R–R, and a <b>constant PR</b> on every conducted beat with a <b>sudden non-conducted P</b> = <b>second-degree AV block, Mobitz II</b>. Compare Mobitz I (Wenckebach), where the PR lengthens progressively before the drop and the PR after the drop is shorter.<br><br>Why the difference matters (Dr. Qazi): <b>Mobitz I is benign</b> (AV nodal, usually no treatment). <b>Mobitz II is high-grade</b>: the block is below the AV node (His–Purkinje) and can progress abruptly to complete heart block → <b>permanent pacemaker</b>, especially in a symptomatic elderly patient.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Mobitz II (constant PR with sudden dropped QRS complexes) is infranodal and high-grade, and it needs a permanent pacemaker. Mobitz I is usually benign.",
wrong={0:"Adenosine blocks AV nodal conduction to terminate or diagnose supraventricular tachycardia. It would worsen a heart block.",
1:"A β-blocker slows AV conduction and heart rate further, which is dangerous in a patient who already has block and near-syncope.",
3:"Reassurance fits <b>Mobitz I</b> in an asymptomatic person. Mobitz II with near-syncope risks progression to complete heart block.",
4:"Caffeine causes premature beats and tachycardia, not dropped QRS complexes. Stopping it will not fix an infranodal block."},
eli5="Every so often the signal from the top chambers just fails to reach the bottom chambers, with no warning. That kind of block sits low in the wiring and can suddenly get much worse. In an older man who nearly fainted, he needs a pacemaker."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 45-year-old woman is being treated in the hospital for pneumonia with intravenous levofloxacin. On day 2, a routine ECG shows sinus rhythm at 94/min. The QT interval measures 440 ms, and the R–R interval is 0.64 seconds.{FIG}Using Bazett's formula, which of the following is her corrected QT interval (QTc)?",
choices=["282 ms","352 ms","440 ms","550 ms","688 ms"], key=3,
exp="<b>Bazett: QTc = QT ÷ √RR</b> (RR in seconds). √0.64 = 0.8 → 440 ÷ 0.8 = <b>550 ms</b>.<br><br>Normal QTc is under <b>450 ms (men)</b> or <b>460 ms (women)</b>. Over <b>500 ms</b> the risk of torsades de pointes rises sharply. Her fluoroquinolone is a known QT-prolonging drug, as are antidepressants, antipsychotics, HIV drugs, dofetilide and sotalol. Dr. Qazi's cautionary story: a patient died of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia after a new fluoroquinolone. Check the medication list and electrolytes (potassium, magnesium) before prescribing, and consider switching antibiotics here.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> QTc = QT ÷ √RR. A QTc over 500 ms markedly raises the risk of torsades de pointes, and fluoroquinolones are common culprits.",
wrong={0:"282 ms multiplies QT by RR (440 × 0.64) instead of dividing by the square root.",
1:"352 ms multiplies QT by √RR (440 × 0.8). The formula <b>divides</b> by √RR.",
2:"440 ms is the <b>uncorrected</b> QT. At a heart rate of 94 the QT must be corrected upward.",
4:"688 ms divides QT by RR (440 ÷ 0.64) without taking the square root."},
eli5="The QT naturally shortens when the heart beats fast, so you correct it for heart rate. Divide the QT by the square root of the time between beats: 440 divided by 0.8 is 550. Anything over 500 is dangerous, and her antibiotic may be to blame."),

dict(img='Lhypok', cap='12-lead ECG with lead II rhythm strip',
stem="A 22-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of generalized weakness and muscle cramps after 5 days of repeated vomiting. Her pulse is 84/min and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Her ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following serum abnormalities is most likely?",
choices=["Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia"], key=3,
exp="Low, flat T waves with a <b>prominent U wave</b> (best seen in V2–V3), sometimes with slight ST depression, is the signature of <b>hypokalemia</b>. Her serum potassium was 1.8 mmol/L after prolonged vomiting (loss of gastric acid → metabolic alkalosis → renal potassium wasting).<br><br>The merged T–U wave makes the QT look long (really a QU interval). Low potassium, like low magnesium, raises the risk of torsades de pointes. Dr. Qazi's contrast: <b>high K</b> → peaked T waves, then everything widens (PR, QRS). <b>Low K</b> → flat T, U waves.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hypokalemia flattens the T waves and produces prominent U waves (an apparently long QT). Hyperkalemia peaks the T waves and widens the QRS.",
wrong={0:"Hypercalcemia <b>shortens</b> the QT (a short ST segment) without producing U waves.",
1:"Hyperkalemia gives the opposite: <b>tall, peaked</b> T waves, then loss of P waves and a wide QRS.",
2:"Hypermagnesemia causes conduction slowing and hypotension, not flat T waves with U waves, and it doesn't follow vomiting.",
4:"Hyponatremia causes confusion and seizures, but it does not produce characteristic ECG changes."},
eli5="All that vomiting washed out her potassium. Low potassium flattens the T wave and adds an extra small bump after it called a U wave. High potassium does the opposite and makes T waves tall and pointy."),

dict(img='krbbb', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 70-year-old man has an ECG at a routine visit. He has no symptoms and takes no medications. His pulse is 78/min. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes this ECG?",
choices=["First-degree AV block","Left anterior fascicular block","Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block","Wolff–Parkinson–White pattern"], key=3,
exp="This is class ECG 2. Wide QRS (over 3 small boxes, ≥120 ms) + <b>rSR′ (\"rabbit ears\") in V1–V2</b> + <b>slurred S waves in I, aVL and V5–V6</b> = <b>right bundle branch block</b>. The mnemonic is \"MaRRoW\": M in V1, W in V6.<br><br>Dr. Qazi's read: sinus ~78, a leftward-leaning axis, and RBBB. The axis was not convincingly left enough to add left anterior fascicular block. Remember that RBBB on its own has a <b>normal</b> axis. Anatomy: His → right bundle + left bundle, and the left bundle splits into anterior and posterior fascicles. A blocked right bundle means the right ventricle is activated late, from the left, which creates the terminal R′ in V1.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right bundle branch block is a QRS of 120 ms or more with an rSR′ in V1–V2 and slurred lateral S waves, with a normal axis.",
wrong={0:"First-degree block needs a PR over 200 ms (more than one big box). His PR is not prolonged.",
1:"Left anterior fascicular block gives a clear left axis with a <b>normal</b> QRS width. It does not produce an rSR′ in V1 or a wide QRS.",
2:"Left bundle branch block makes V1–V3 mostly <b>negative</b> with broad notched R waves in I, aVL and V6 (\"WiLLiaM\").",
4:"Wolff–Parkinson–White shows a <b>short PR</b> (under 120 ms) with a slurred QRS upstroke (delta wave). Here the PR is normal and the slurring is at the end of the QRS."},
eli5="The right branch of the heart's wiring is blocked, so the right side gets its signal late, by a detour from the left. That late signal makes a second bump in V1, called rabbit ears. It also widens the QRS."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 59-year-old woman with long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease comes to the office for follow-up. An ECG shows sinus rhythm at 88/min. In lead II, the P wave is tall and pointed, measuring 3 mm in height and 100 ms in duration. In V1, the P wave is mostly positive, with a large initial upward deflection. The QRS voltages are normal.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely ECG diagnosis?",
choices=["Hyperkalemia","Left atrial enlargement","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right atrial enlargement","Wolff–Parkinson–White pattern"], key=3,
exp="Atrial enlargement is read from the <b>P wave in lead II and V1</b>. The normal P is right atrium first, then left atrium: upright in II and biphasic in V1.<br>• <b>Right atrial enlargement</b>: a <b>tall, peaked P in II</b> (over 2.5 mm, \"P pulmonale\") and a <b>mostly positive P in V1</b> (a big initial part, because the right atrium depolarizes first). It is typical of chronic lung disease raising right-sided pressures. In children, think congenital heart disease.<br>• Left atrial enlargement: a <b>wide</b> (over 120 ms), notched P in II (\"P mitrale\") and a <b>mostly negative</b> P in V1 (big terminal part).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Right atrial enlargement is a tall peaked P in II (over 2.5 mm) with a mostly positive P in V1. Left atrial enlargement is a wide notched P in II with a mostly negative P in V1.",
wrong={0:"Hyperkalemia peaks the <b>T wave</b>, not the P wave, and it eventually flattens or erases the P waves.",
1:"Left atrial enlargement makes the P <b>wide</b> (over 120 ms) and notched in II and <b>mostly negative</b> in V1. Her P is tall, narrow and positive in V1.",
2:"Left ventricular hypertrophy is diagnosed from QRS voltage and axis, and her QRS voltages are normal.",
4:"Wolff–Parkinson–White is a short PR with a delta wave in the QRS. It does not change P-wave height."},
eli5="Her lung disease strains the right side of the heart, so the right upper chamber has grown. The right atrium makes the first part of the P wave, so the P wave becomes tall and pointy. In V1 it points mostly upward."),

dict(img=None,
stem="A 64-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 1 hour of chest pressure radiating to his left arm. His pulse is 84/min and blood pressure is 138/82 mm Hg. An ECG shows 2–3 mm of ST-segment elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, with reciprocal ST depression in II, III, and aVF. Leads V1 through V4 show no ST elevation.{FIG}Occlusion of which of the following arteries is most likely responsible?",
choices=["Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery","Posterior descending artery","Proximal left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery"], key=0,
exp="ST elevation in <b>I, aVL, V5 and V6</b> = the <b>lateral wall</b>, with reciprocal inferior depression confirming a STEMI.<br><br>Dr. Qazi's coronary table: the <b>left circumflex</b> runs in the posterior AV groove and gives the obtuse marginal branches to the <b>lateral and inferolateral wall</b>. The lecture's lead map gives I, aVL and V5–V6 → circumflex (or a diagonal branch). With V1–V4 spared, the LAD territory (anteroseptal and anterior) is not involved, which points to the circumflex.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> ST elevation confined to I, aVL, V5 and V6 localizes to the lateral wall, most often a left circumflex artery occlusion.",
wrong={1:"Left main occlusion would involve both the LAD and circumflex territories, with widespread anterior and lateral changes (often ST elevation in aVR), usually with shock.",
2:"The posterior descending artery supplies the <b>inferior</b> wall, giving ST elevation in II, III and aVF. These leads show depression here.",
3:"Proximal LAD occlusion would elevate the ST segments across <b>V1–V4</b> as well as I and aVL. V1–V4 are normal.",
4:"Right coronary occlusion causes an <b>inferior</b> STEMI (II, III, aVF), which is the opposite of this pattern."},
eli5="The leads that look at the left side wall of the heart show the heart-attack bumps. The front-wall leads are normal. The side wall is fed mainly by the circumflex artery, so that's the blocked one."),

dict(img='kchbstemi', cap='12-lead ECG with rhythm strips',
stem="A 68-year-old man is brought to the emergency department because of 1 hour of crushing chest pain and lightheadedness. His pulse is 36/min and blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg. His ECG is shown.{FIG}Occlusion of which of the following arteries best explains both the rhythm and the ST changes?",
choices=["Diagonal branch of the LAD","Left circumflex artery","Left main coronary artery","Proximal left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery"], key=4,
exp="This is class ECG 3. Two findings, one cause:<br>• <b>Rhythm</b>: P–P regular, R–R regular (6 × 6 = 36/min), PR varying at random → <b>third-degree (complete) heart block</b> with a narrow junctional escape rhythm.<br>• <b>Injury</b>: ST elevation in <b>II, III, aVF</b> with reciprocal depression in I and aVL → <b>acute inferior STEMI</b>.<br><br>In a right-dominant circulation (~80%), the <b>right coronary artery</b> gives the posterior descending artery (inferior wall) and supplies the <b>AV node (~90%)</b> and the SA node (~60%). So a right coronary artery occlusion infarcts the inferior wall <b>and</b> makes the AV node ischemic, causing heart block. Heart block from an inferior infarct is often transient (AV nodal) but may need temporary pacing.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Inferior STEMI with complete heart block points to right coronary artery occlusion, because the right coronary artery supplies both the inferior wall and the AV node in most people.",
wrong={0:"A diagonal branch supplies the anterolateral wall (I, aVL). Those leads show ST <b>depression</b> here.",
1:"The circumflex can supply the inferior wall in left-dominant hearts (~20%), but it supplies the AV node far less often. Here the combination of inferior STEMI and heart block points to the right coronary artery.",
2:"Left main occlusion causes a massive anterior and lateral injury pattern with shock, not an isolated inferior STEMI.",
3:"Proximal LAD occlusion causes an <b>anterior</b> STEMI (V1–V4, I, aVL). When it causes conduction trouble, it is usually a bundle branch block below the node, not AV nodal block with a narrow escape rhythm."},
eli5="One artery, the right coronary, feeds both the bottom wall of the heart and the AV node that passes signals down. When it clogged, the bottom wall showed heart-attack changes. The signals also stopped getting through, so the heart dropped to a slow backup rhythm."),

dict(img='Lwpw', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 25-year-old man comes to the office because of several episodes of sudden, rapid palpitations over the past year that start and stop abruptly. He feels well today. His pulse is 72/min. His ECG is shown.{FIG}The abnormality on this ECG is best explained by which of the following?",
choices=["Delayed conduction through the right bundle branch","Early activation via an accessory pathway","Enhanced automaticity of a ventricular ectopic focus","Prolonged repolarization of ventricular myocytes","Slowed conduction through the AV node"], key=1,
exp="A <b>short PR</b> (under 120 ms) + a <b>slurred QRS upstroke (delta wave)</b> + a slightly wide QRS with secondary ST–T changes = <b>ventricular pre-excitation</b>, the Wolff–Parkinson–White pattern. With paroxysmal tachycardia it is <b>Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome</b>.<br><br>An <b>accessory pathway</b> (bundle of Kent) connects atrium and ventricle directly, skipping the AV nodal delay → part of the ventricle is activated early and slowly, muscle to muscle (the delta wave) → the rest arrives through the normal His–Purkinje system → a fusion QRS. The loop through pathway and node can sustain re-entrant tachycardia. The notes cover it as a board add-on, with arrhythmias taught in a later lecture.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> The delta wave of Wolff–Parkinson–White is early, slow ventricular activation through an accessory pathway that bypasses the AV node, which also shortens the PR.",
wrong={0:"Right bundle branch delay widens the <b>end</b> of the QRS (the rSR′ in V1) and leaves the PR normal. It does not slur the initial upstroke or shorten the PR.",
2:"A ventricular ectopic focus produces wide premature beats or ventricular tachycardia. It does not produce a short PR on every sinus beat.",
3:"Prolonged repolarization is <b>long QT</b>, which affects the T wave, not the start of the QRS.",
4:"Slowed AV nodal conduction <b>lengthens</b> the PR (first-degree block). His PR is short."},
eli5="He was born with an extra electrical shortcut between the upper and lower heart chambers. Part of the signal sneaks through early, so the PR is short and the QRS starts with a slow ramp called the delta wave. The shortcut can also let signals loop round and cause racing spells."),
]
